== 6. FINDING (advisory, new, arising from the remedy) — the dropped emit has no regression guard ==
I re-added the REAL emit — the exact code a12d43e removed:
    if (target.kind === "sella") {
      emitEvent(root, manifest, "workflow.actor_assigned", now, { [WF.ITEM_ID]: target.id, [WF.ACTOR_ROLE]: target.value });
    }
and ran every plausible guard. NOTHING caught it:
  delegate.test.ts behaviour 7   rc=0  0 FAIL
  delegate.test.ts (whole file)  rc=0  0 FAIL
  apps/server/test/server.test.ts rc=0  0 FAIL
  packages/core/src/index.test.ts rc=0  0 FAIL
The amended brief makes this a SPECIFICATION ("Neither shape emits an event ... this paragraph
is the specification for that drop"). A specification with no assertion is prose, and prose is
what the standing rule says enforcement must never be. The fix itself is correct and measured
correct (section 4); what is missing is the guard that keeps it correct.
Advisory, not blocking, and deliberately so: the amendment's nine instructions did not ask for
this assertion, so the builder skipped no instruction, and blocking a further round on a guard
the spec gate did not require would be the censor legislating past the architect. Cost to close
is one line in behaviour 7, which already builds a fresh studio and runs a successful delegate:
  check(7, "no workflow.* event is emitted by either shape", !existsSync(join(dir, ".bisellium", "events.jsonl")));
RECOMMEND filing it rather than carrying it as a memory.

== 7. deviation 1 — the 5.2s real-time TTL wait: ACCEPTABLE, but the seam does exist ==
server.test.ts now does `await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 5_200))` to cross
MODELS_LISTING_TTL_MS (5_000, a module-level const in http.ts closed over by createListingCache).
The assertion it buys is real: mutant 30b is KILLED, measured.
Measured cost: apps/server/test/server.test.ts wall time 10.30s, of which 5.2s is pure sleep —
more than half that file's runtime, paid on every `npm test`, permanently, for one boolean.
The builder's "no fake-timer seam existed" is true of the code as written, but the seam is
already the house pattern of the very interface being used. StartServerOptions carries FIVE
optional injected knobs today:
  studioDir · port? · pollMs? · now? · live? · checkStudio · runners · listModels?
`pollMs?` is already a millisecond timing knob and `now?` is already a clock knob — and
apps/server/src/store.ts's StoreOptions.now documents itself as "Pinned clock, for reproducible
query results in tests". A `listingTtlMs?: number` defaulting to MODELS_LISTING_TTL_MS is one
optional field, matches two existing precedents in the same options object, and turns 5.2s into
~20ms. Ruling: acceptable for this gate (the test is correct and the mutant dies); advisory,
with the seam named precisely so it is not reported again as "none existed".

== 8. deviation 2 — the playwright sudo failure: PRE-EXISTING, environmental, not W-065's ==
The brief's Acceptance line `npx playwright install --with-deps chromium` exits 1 here:
  Error: Installation process exited with code: 1
`--with-deps` shells out to apt-get and needs root, which this sandbox does not have. It is
unrunnable in this environment and always will be. It does not affect the substantive claim the
line exists to enable: chromium-1243 is cached, and from a clean `rm -rf apps/web/dist` rebuild
both suites pass — test:e2e 5/5, test:serve 16/16. Not a defect of this opus.

== 9. the 05.log reading: CORRECT ANSWER, WEAKER REASONING THAN IT DESERVES ==
Verified first that the claim is true: `git log -- studio/ci/reds/W-065/05.log` -> last touched
at 1c65813; `git diff --stat 1c65813 HEAD -- studio/ci/reds/W-065/` shows only 14.log and 15.log
(added at 4f46a31, round 1). No red was re-recorded or edited in round 2.
The builder's stated reason is an ARGUMENT FROM SILENCE — it searched the amended brief for
language authorizing a re-record and found none. That is the wrong test: absence of permission
is not the same as presence of a reason, and the same search would have justified the opposite
answer had it found a stray sentence.
The affirmative reasons, which is what should have been written:
  (a) this repo's red UNIT is the behaviour, not the case — one ci/reds/W-065/NN.log per
      --behaviour N. Behaviour 5 already has a genuine, assertion-level red at 1c65813, and the
      amendment re-worded behaviour 5's case list without adding a behaviour (still 17).
  (b) decisively: a red recorded TODAY for behaviour 5 could only be manufactured by reverting
      working code. That is not a recorded red, it is backfilled evidence, which the standing
      rule forbids outright. The absence of authorizing language is beside the point; the
      presence of a rule against it is the point.
  (c) the thing a red is FOR — proof the new fixture discriminates — I produced instead by
      mutation, live, in section 1: disable hasMergeKey and the alias-free fixture passes.
Ruling: the reading is upheld. 05.log stays. The builder reached the right answer; the next
round should reach it by rule rather than by search.

